842 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (The Freedom in Africa Report \(FinAR 2025\) examines African countries' performance in three key areas that together define freedom and progress: Political Freedom, Economic Freedom, and Prosperity. It measures how open and fair governments are, how easy it is for people to do business or find work, and how healthy citizens live in terms of education, health, and opportunity. )
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [3 4]
>>
endobj
843 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Each country is rated on a scale from 0 to 100, where 100 represents the highest level of freedom and well-being.)
/Pg 11 0 R
>>
endobj
844 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E <>
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
845 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/T (Northern Africa \(Score: 51.2\) Socially advanced, politically closed)
/E (Northern Africa \(Score: 51.2\) Socially advanced, politically closed)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
846 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Northern Africa tells a story of promise held back by politics. The region has significantly improved education, healthcare, and infrastructure; people live longer, literacy is higher, and cities are expanding fast. Yet, citizens still live under tight political control. Governments across the region restrict protests, regulate the media, and limit free speech.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
847 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (The Arab Maghreb Union \(AMU\), created in 1989 to boost trade and cooperation among Morocco, Algeria, Tunisia, Libya, and Mauritania, is still mostly inactive. Political rivalry between Morocco and Algeria, especially over Western Sahara, has frozen most regional plans. As a result, trade among these countries makes up less than 5% of their total trade, one of the lowest levels of regional integration anywhere on the continent.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
848 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Tunisia, once a symbol of hope after the Arab Spring, has reversed many of its democratic gains since 2021. Egypt, Algeria, and Libya remain under firm government control, where dissent is often punished or silenced.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
849 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Still, Northern Africa is not without strengths. Its education systems are among the best on the continent, and it leads in access to electricity and digital connectivity. However, youth unemployment remains high, around 30%, and most economies depend heavily on oil and gas exports. Currency instability and high living costs add to public frustration.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
850 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E <>
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
851 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/T (West Africa \(Score: 57.8\) Mobile, vocal, and politically restless)
/E (West Africa \(Score: 57.8\) Mobile, vocal, and politically restless)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
852 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E <>
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [13]
>>
endobj
853 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (But this openness coexists with political instability. Since 2020, six military coups have taken place in Mali, Burkina Faso, Niger, and Guinea. These coups also led to the creation of the Alliance of Sahel States \(AES\) in 2024, signalling a breakaway from ECOWAS and weakening its influence.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [14]
>>
endobj
854 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (At the same time, West Africa has a young, outspoken population that refuses to stay silent. From the #EndSARS protests in Nigeria to democracy marches in Senegal, young people are demanding jobs, transparency, and better governance. They are frustrated by corruption, poor infrastructure, and inflation that eats away at their earnings.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [15]
>>
endobj
855 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E <>
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [16 17]
>>
endobj
856 0 obj
<<
